  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Just energy transition through scaling modern clean cooking fuels and technologies in Burkina Faso</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Burkina Faso is confronted with significant obstacles in advancing its climate action goals and providing affordable, clean energy, both critical to its development strategy. The country faces severe impacts from climate change, including extreme weather events, prolonged droughts, and unpredictable rainfall. These conditions have a particularly harsh effect on agriculture, which is the backbone of the economy (The agricultural sector accounts for 35% of the country&apos;s gross domestic product (GDP) and employs 82% of the working population.). Although Burkina Faso has made some progress, it must intensify its efforts to meet commitments under the Paris Agreement and the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), specifically SDG 7 (Affordable and Clean Energy) and SDG 13 (Climate Action). The nation has set a target to reduce its greenhouse gas emissions by 11.6% by 2030, with a more ambitious goal of a 29.4% reduction, contingent upon international support . These targets focus on key sectors such as agriculture, forestry, and energy.  A significant challenge is Burkina Faso&apos;s severe land degradation compounded by deforestation occurring at a rate of 105,000 hectares per year  This degradation diminishes biodiversity, accelerates soil erosion, and decreases agricultural productivity, which threatens fo</narrative>
  </description>
